(*
Issue #550: Parser::parsePower's own right-associative recursion for a
'**'/'pow' chain (EP section 6.8.3.2) does not funnel through parseFactor,
so it was never covered by ExprDepth/MaxExprDepth the way every other
recursive re-entry into expression parsing is -- it drove the real C++
call stack directly, with no ceiling at all. 100,000 terms is the
issue's own reproduction scale (empirically: 50,000 terms parsed to
completion and was correctly rejected by Sema's own MaxExprDepth=1000
before this fix; 100,000 reliably overflowed the stack during PARSING
itself, before Sema ever got a turn). Fixed by comparing live stack
headroom (llvm::getStackPointer()/getDefaultStackSize(),
Parser::StackBaseline) against the platform's real budget at parsePower's
own recursive call site, rather than a term-count ceiling -- see
powerStackNearlyExhausted's own comment (ParseExpr.cpp) for why. Checked
in verbatim rather than generated at build time -- lit has no loop-emitting
primitive, and the source is deterministic; nobody should have to look at
100,000 '** 2' terms to read this file, so the lit directives come first.

A crash surfaces as a real signal (SIGSEGV etc.), not just a nonzero exit
code -- plain `not` (without --crash) already distinguishes the two, the
same way every sibling test in this directory does.
*)

(*
RUN: not %plang -std=iso10206 %s -o %t 2> %t.err
RUN: FileCheck %s < %t.err
*)

(*
CHECK: nested too deeply
*)

